-- Import of seven captures that were logged to disk but never transmitted.
--
--   npx wrangler d1 execute orb-wave --remote --file=./migrations/0007_import_logged_runs.sql
--
-- Data only - no schema change - and re-runnable: every INSERT is OR IGNORE, so a
-- second run writes nothing rather than failing on the primary key.
--
-- WHY these seven. They are the most drifting measurements in the archive, and
-- none of them were in the database: net drift runs 116-780 m here against a
-- best of 774 m and a median under 20 m for everything already stored. The
-- minimum-drift filter was therefore filtering a set that did not contain the
-- rows it was built to find. Source: Målinger/m-linger/, folders 20260726 and
-- 20260731 Skjærhalden.
--
-- WHAT IS DIFFERENT about a file-imported row, all of it deliberate:
--
--   device     'file:<folder>/<run>' rather than a Notehub UID. This is the only
--              mark of provenance, since buoy_id is the real buoy (1245204) -
--              these are its earlier deployments, not a different instrument.
--              SELECT ... WHERE device LIKE 'file:%' separates them again.
--   received   set to ts_end. Nothing received these; the dashboard's time filter
--              runs on this column, so leaving it null was not an option and any
--              other value would put a July capture in the wrong time slice.
--   reading_id NULL. This generation of ses.csv does not record one, and inventing
--              a counter would collide with the ids the radio path assigns.
--   tp         NULL. ana.csv of this generation carries Hs, Tz and Tc only. The
--              dashboard computes its own Tp from the spectrum, so these rows show
--              a value under "Tp" and a dash under "Tp, buoy" - which is exactly
--              the truth about them.
--   rssi       NULL. There was no radio link to measure.
--
-- POSITIONS come from the run's own gps.csv: the first and last fix with a 3D lock
-- and four or more satellites. Not row 0 - a receiver's opening rows routinely
-- predate its first lock, and those would put the window's start wherever it was
-- still guessing. The counts below say how many of the file's rows survived that.
--
-- THE SPECTRUM comes from spec.csv's psd_acc_madgwick column, at the f_hz stored
-- with it, and psd is DERIVED here as psd_acc / (2*pi*f)^4 - the same line the
-- Worker runs on an incoming message (worker.js:344), rounded the same way.
--
-- The psd_eta_madgwick column sitting right there in the file is deliberately NOT
-- used, and this is the one trap in the whole import. That column is the drifter's
-- own working value, with its kTaperF1/kTaperF2 already folded in as taper^2. Put
-- it in wave_bin.psd and the dashboard would taper a spectrum that was already
-- tapered: every Hs, Tp, Tm01 and Tm02 on these rows would read low, and would
-- move differently from every other row as the taper pickers change. Untapered in
-- the database, tapered only by the page - the same contract the radio path keeps.


